[tool_use] Agent {"description":"Review Phase 2b.2 implementation","subagent_type":"code-reviewer","prompt":"Review the Phase 2b.2 implementation in this repo. Phase 2b.2 swaps the 2b.1 manual SDP paste for QR-code pairing. The phase is gated by docs/QUALITY_GATES.md §\"Browser-API limitations (Phase 2b)\" — WebRTC handshakes are deferred to manual real-device QA; pure logic must be unit-tested.\n\nContext you should read:\n- docs/PHASES.md §\"Phase 2b.2: QR-code pairing flow\" — acceptance criteria.\n- artifacts/decision-memo.md §\"Sub-phase 2b.2 — QR-code pairing flow\" — planner intent, especially the \"throwaway-vs-fallback\" treatment of the 2b.1 manual UI.\n- docs/QUALITY_GATES.md §\"Browser-API limitations (Phase 2b)\" — the testing-gate concession.\n- artifacts/phase-approval.json — the 2b.1 approval that established the §B2 concession pattern.\n\nFiles added/changed in this phase:\n- app/src/phone/qr.ts (new) — gzip+base64url encode/decode for the offer/answer fragment, with an `oversize` flag when the compressed payload exceeds 1200 bytes (one QR @ V25 EC-L).\n- app/src/phone/qr.test.ts (new) — 7 unit tests: round-trip, base64url charset, oversize detection, malformed b64, malformed gzip, byte-length surfacing.\n- app/src/phone/pair-url.ts (new) — pure helpers: `buildPhoneUrl(parts, offerPayload | null)` and `parsePhoneFragment(hash)`.\n- app/src/phone/pair-url.test.ts (new) — 10 unit tests for URL/fragment edge cases.\n- app/src/ui/PairScreen.tsx (rewritten) — adds QR mode with three states (preparing-offer / showing-offer / scanning-answer / connected / error), webcam scan loop via jsqr + requestAnimationFrame, manual fallback toggle that surfaces the 2b.1 textarea UI, auto-fallback on oversize SDP / camera denied / missing LAN IP.\n- app/src/phone/PhoneApp.tsx (rewritten) — auto-applies offer from URL fragment, renders answer QR via qrcode lib, falls back to manual paste on every error case (b64, gzip, oversize answer, acceptOffer throw).\n- app/src/main.tsx — wraps PhoneApp in React.lazy + Suspense so the laptop main bundle never imports PhoneApp.\n- app/src/App.tsx — wraps PairScreen in React.lazy + Suspense.\n- app/src/ui/SettingsScreen.tsx — minor text update (removed \"QR arrives in 2b.2\" hint).\n- app/verify-phase-2b2.mjs (new) — Playwright verify script with 3 gate-grade checks (C1, C3, C5) + 2 best-effort checks (C2, C4) that hit the same WebRTC/ICE limitation as 2b.1 C5, plus a 6-item manual QA checklist (M1–M6).\n- app/package.json — added jsqr@^1.4.0, qrcode@^1.5.4 deps, @types/qrcode@^1.5.6 devDep.\n- docs/PHASES.md — marked §2b.2 as \"✅ shipped\" and corrected the URL fragment format docs to `#offer=<payload>`.\n\nVerify-script results (artifacts/phase-2b2-verify/results.json):\n- C1 PASS: /phone without fragment → manual paste UI.\n- C2 FAIL (expected, best-effort): /phone#offer=<valid> never renders answer QR in headless chromium because peer.acceptOffer awaits ICE complete, which doesn't happen without UDP/STUN. Documented under §B2.\n- C3 PASS: /phone#offer=!!! → manual fallback banner with \"QR payload could not be read\" message.\n- C4 FAIL (expected, best-effort): Pair view's offer QR depends on peer.createOffer + ICE complete — same hang.\n- C5 PASS: PairScreen \"Show manual pairing\" toggle reveals the 2b.1 textareas.\n- console_errors: 0.\n\nGates run locally:\n- npm run lint → 0 errors.\n- npm run typecheck → 0 errors.\n- npm test → 147 tests pass (up from 130 in 2b.1; +7 qr.ts, +10 pair-url.ts).\n- npm run build → main bundle 344.20 KB / 106.45 KB gzip (Phase 2b.1 baseline was 349.80 / 107.55) — main shrank by 1.10 KB gzip because PhoneApp moved to a lazy chunk. PairScreen chunk 136.89 KB / 50.17 KB gzip (lazy-loaded). PhoneApp chunk 4.41 KB / 1.67 KB gzip. +0 KB main budget satisfied.\n\nReview for: correctness bugs (especially in the qr.ts encode/decode path and the PairScreen/PhoneApp state machines), missed-error-paths (what happens if jsqr returns garbage that decodes as base64 but isn't a real SDP — does PairScreen keep scanning? answer: yes, by design — confirm this is correct), DRY/duplication (PhoneApp and PairScreen both have similar peer wiring; is the duplication ok or should it be extracted), readability, security (the URL fragment is base64-encoded gzipped SDP — no auth, but §B1 of the decision memo justifies this as the LAN trust model), performance (scan loop runs at requestAnimationFrame — ~60fps — is this OK or should it throttle), and whether the testing gate is satisfied given the §B2 concession.\n\nReturn APPROVE / APPROVE-WITH-CHANGES / REQUEST-CHANGES with a numbered list of findings, each tagged blocking | non-blocking | nit. Be concrete about file:line. Stay under ~1000 words."}
